---
alwaysApply: true
---
You can keep the folder layout we just defined and wire it up with Bun’s native workspaces and scripts.

Root workspace config (Bun)
Bun treats workspaces via the workspaces field in the root package.json, very similar to npm/yarn.

text
// package.json (root)
{
  "name": "melon-monorepo",
  "private": true,
  "workspaces": [
    "apps/*",
    "packages/*"
  ],
  "scripts": {
    "install": "bun install",
    "dev": "bun run --filter './apps/*' dev",
    "build": "bun run --filter './packages/*' build",
    "test": "bun run --filter './packages/*' test",
    "lint": "bun run --filter './packages/*' lint",
    "typecheck": "bun run --filter './packages/*' typecheck"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "bun-types": "latest",
    "typescript": "latest",
    "turbo": "latest"
  }
}
workspaces matches Bun’s monorepo docs.

The bun run --filter pattern is from the Bun docs and monorepo examples; it will run the script in each workspace that implements it.

From the root, you’ll run:

bash
bun install       # installs all workspace deps
bun run dev       # runs dev where defined (e.g. apps/*)
Workspace dependency wiring
In each package, use the "workspace:*" version syntax so Bun treats them as local links.

Example for @melon-db/db-react:

text
// packages/melon-db-react/package.json
{
  "name": "@melon-db/db-react",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "main": "dist/index.js",
  "types": "dist/index.d.ts",
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "bun run src/index.ts",
    "build": "bun build ./src/index.ts --outdir ./dist --target node",
    "test": "bun test",
    "lint": "bunx eslint src --ext .ts,.tsx",
    "typecheck": "bunx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "@melon-db/db": "workspace:*",
    "react": "^18.0.0"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "bun-types": "latest",
    "typescript": "latest"
  }
}
Example for @melon-db/db-sqlite:

text
// packages/melon-db-sqlite/package.json
{
  "name": "@melon-db/db-sqlite",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "main": "dist/index.js",
  "types": "dist/index.d.ts",
  "scripts": {
    "build": "bun build ./src/index.ts --outdir ./dist --target node",
    "test": "bun test",
    "lint": "bunx eslint src --ext .ts",
    "typecheck": "bunx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "@melon-db/db": "workspace:*"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "bun-types": "latest",
    "typescript": "latest"
  }
}
This follows Bun’s documented "workspace:*" pattern for linking local packages.

Example app wired to packages
For apps/playground-rn:

text
// apps/playground-rn/package.json
{
  "name": "playground-rn",
  "private": true,
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "bun run expo start",         // or `bun run react-native start`
    "build": "bun run expo build",
    "test": "bun test"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "@melon-db/db": "workspace:*",
    "@melon-db/db-sqlite": "workspace:*",
    "@melon-db/db-react": "workspace:*",
    "react": "18.x",
    "react-native": "0.7x.x"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "bun-types": "latest",
    "typescript": "latest"
  }
}
From the root, bun run dev will discover dev under apps/playground-rn thanks to --filter.

Optional: Turborepo + Bun
If you want build/test orchestration, you can still use Turborepo with Bun as the runner, like the examples combining Turborepo and Bun workspaces.

Example turbo.json:

text
{
  "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
  "pipeline": {
    "build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**"]
    },
    "test": {
      "dependsOn": ["build"]
    },
    "lint": {},
    "typecheck": {}
  }
}
Then in root package.json:

text
"scripts": {
  "turbo": "turbo",
  "build": "turbo run build --filter=...",
  "test": "turbo run test",
  "lint": "turbo run lint",
  "typecheck": "turbo run typecheck"
}
You still install everything with bun install; Turborepo just coordinates tasks.

Summary of boundaries with Bun
Root: workspaces: ["apps/*", "packages/*"] and bun install per Bun’s docs.

Each package: name, version, scripts, and "@melon-db/*": "workspace:*" dependencies.

You’ll generally run bun run --filter from root to spawn dev/build/test across workspaces.